        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Discovery of Neutron-Induced Radioactivity</strong><br>\nFermi's most significant contribution came in the form of neutron-induced radioactivity, discovered in 1934. By bombarding elements with neutrons, he and his team were able to create new elements, leading to the realization that nuclear reactions could be used to transform one element into another. This discovery was pivotal to the later development of atomic energy and weapons.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel Prize and Move to America</strong><br>\nFermi was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1938 for his work on the artificial radioactivity produced by neutrons. Shortly after, due to the rise of fascism in Italy and Fermi's antifascist views, along with his Jewish wife's peril, he immigrated to the United States where he became a key figure in American physics and the developing atomic age.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Manhattan Project</strong><br>\nDuring World War II, Fermi played a crucial role in the Manhattan Project, the secret US government research project aimed at building the first atomic bombs. He led the team that achieved the first controlled nuclear chain reaction under the stands of Stagg Field at the University of Chicago on December 2, 1942.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
