          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Post-War Work and Education</strong><br>\nAfter the war, Fermi continued his work as a professor at the Institute for Nuclear Studies at the University of Chicago. He was deeply involved in teaching and research, striving to understand the fundamental particles of nature and the forces governing them. Fermi was known for his dedication to his students and his belief in the importance of scientific education.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Contributions to Particle Physics</strong><br>\nBeyond nuclear physics, Fermi's interest extended to the then-emerging field of particle physics. He theorized the existence of a particle later called the neutrino and worked on the theory of beta decay. Fermi's methods and theoretical frameworks are still used by particle physicists today.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nEnrico Fermi died on November 28, 1954, in Chicago, Illinois. His death was a loss to the scientific community, but his legacy lived on. Fermi's name is commemorated in the Fermi National Accelerator Laboratory (Fermilab) and in the element fermium. He is remembered as one of the most important physicists of the 20th century, having laid the foundations for nuclear power and atomic theory.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
